# Galactic cosmic rays in the heliosphere S.E.S. Ferreira \*, M.S. Potgieter Unit for Space Physics, School of Physics, Potchefstroom University for CHE, 2520 Potchefstroom, South Africa Received 21 November 2002; received in revised form 19 February 2003; accepted 19 February 2003 # Abstract Galactic cosmic rays entering the heliosphere are subjected to four modulation processes: diffusion, convection, adiabatic deceleration in the expanding solar wind, gradient, curvature and current sheet drifts. These particles also encounter the termination shock where they may get re-accelerated via diffusive shock acceleration to reach substantial higher energies. An overview of these processes is presented with emphasis on the outer heliosphere where a considerable amount of modulation may take place, especially in the region between the termination shock and the modulation boundary/heliopause. It is illustrated what can be learned from cosmic ray measurements in the outer heliosphere and from computations with various modulation models about important issues like e.g., the location and modulation effects of the termination shock, modulation in the heliosheath, the size and shape of the heliosphere, the local interstellar spectra at lower energies, and the transport parameters and mechanisms. © 2004 COSPAR. Published by Elsevier Ltd. All rights reserved. Keywords: Heliosphere; Galactic cosmic rays; Diffusion; Convection # 1. Introduction Our nearest star, the Sun, is a rotating magnetic star of which the plasma atmosphere constantly blows radially away from its surface to maintain equilibrium (e.g., Parker, 1958), a phenomenon called the solar wind. The solar wind creates the heliosphere, shown in Fig. 1, which is a bubble of magnetized plasma that surrounds the Sun and includes the orbits of all the planets (see Fichtner and Scherer, 2000, for an overview). However, the interstellar space is not empty and contains matter in the form of the local interstellar medium (LISM). As the heliosphere moves through the LISM it forces the plasma component of the LISM to flow around it. At large radial distances the LISM pressure causes the supersonic solar wind speed to decrease to sub-sonic speeds and a heliospheric shock is created, called the solar wind termination shock (TS). At even larger radial distances a contact surface is eventually reached, called the heliopause, which separates solar from interstellar material. The region between the TS and the heliopause is known as the heliosheath. De pending on the interstellar conditions, there may also even be an outward pointing external bow shock around the heliosphere. Imbedded in the solar wind is the Sun's magnetic field wounded up in a spiral and transported with the solar wind into space forming the heliospheric magnetic field (HMF). The HMF determines the passage of charged particles, such as cosmic rays (CRs), in the heliosphere where it changes their intensities with time as a function of energy and position. This process is known as the modulation of CRs and serves as an indirect probe to study the outer heliospheric regions that cannot yet be accessed directly (e.g., Heber, 2001). In this work an overview of galactic CR modulation will be presented with emphasis on the outer heliosphere where a considerable amount of modulation may take place, especially in the region between the TS and the modulation boundary/heliopause. # 2. Cosmic ray transport inside the heliosphere Galactic cosmic rays (GCRs) originate from far outside our solar system and penetrate the heliosphere to Earth's orbit serving as messengers from the distant ![](images/06638956b051c35aff72d4735dff5f32c89fec6bdcb52d5dc4942c4b8552cc33.jpg)  
Fig. 1. A sketch of the heliosphere in the rest frame of the Sun (Fichtner and Scherer, 2000). outer heliosphere (Lee and Fichtner, 2001) providing valuable information about this region that cannot yet be measured directly. GCRs are charged and mostly fully ionized highly energetic particles with kinetic energy greater than a few hundred keV to as high as $3 \times 10^{21}$ eV. Those that arrive at Earth are composed of $\sim 98\%$ nuclei, stripped of all their orbital electrons, and $\sim 2\%$ electrons and positrons. GCRs are accelerated during supernova explosions (Axford et al., 1977; Bell, 1978; Blandford and Ostriker, 1978) and are distributed in the form of a power law $j \propto E^{\gamma}$ , with $\gamma = 2.6$ the spectral index, $E$ the kinetic energy in MeV/nucleon and; $j = P^2 f$ , the differential intensity typically in units of particles $\mathrm{m}^{-2} \mathrm{s}^{-1} \mathrm{sr}^{-1} \mathrm{MeV}^{-1}$ with $f$ the distribution function (as in Eq. (1)); $P$ is rigidity where $P = pc / q$ with $p$ the particle's momentum, $q$ the charge and $c$ the speed of light. For $E > 10^{15}$ GeV the GCR spectrum breaks and becomes steeper with $\gamma \approx 3.1$ . This break is known as the “knee” of the spectrum due to less efficient acceleration in supernova shocks. For $E \lesssim 20$ GeV, GCRs measured at Earth have $\gamma \neq 2.6$ due to heliospheric modulation effects which can be describe by four major processes: When GCRs enter our heliosphere they are convected with the solar wind outwards away from the Sun. During this process the solar wind expands, as does the “cosmic ray gas” resulting in adiabatic cooling (deceleration). However, CRs are also accelerated in compression regions or at shocks via first order Fermi acceleration. Due to irregularities in the HMF these particles are also scattered so that they effectively undergo a diffusive random walk along and across the HMF lines. CRs may also experience drift motions due to gradients and curvature of the HMF or any abrupt changes in the field direction, such as the heliospheric current sheet (HCS). To describe the heliospheric transport of GCRs, the four major modulation processes were combined by Parker (1965) into a transport equation: $$
\begin{array}{l} \frac {\partial f}{\partial t} = - (\mathbf {V} + \langle \mathbf {v} _ {D} \rangle) \cdot \nabla f + \nabla \cdot (\mathbf {K} _ {\mathrm{s}} \cdot \nabla f) + \frac {1}{3} (\nabla \cdot \mathbf {V}) \\ \times \frac {\partial f}{\partial \ln P} + J _ {\text {source}}, \tag {1} \\ \end{array}
$$ with $f(\mathbf{r}, P, t)$ is the omnidirectional CR distribution function; r is position, V is the solar wind velocity and t is time. Terms on the right-hand side represent convection, gradient, and curvature drifts, diffusion, adiabatic energy changes, and a source function, respectively. This equation contains all the relevant physics to describe CR transport and acceleration in the heliosphere (see Potgieter, 1998, for a detailed discussion) and is generally solved numerically in so-called modulation models, first done in a steady-state $(\partial f/\partial t = 0)$ and for a spherically symmetric heliosphere (1-dimensional) by Fisk (1971). Since then these models have grown progressively in sophistication, such as two-dimensional (2D) steady-state drift models (e.g., Hattingh and Burger, 1995a), time-dependent 2D models to simulate temporal effects (e.g., le Roux and Potgieter, 1995), 3D steady-state models (Hattingh and Burger, 1995b; Zhang, 1999) and 2D acceleration models (e.g., Steenkamp, 1995) and fully 3D time-dependent solutions (e.g., Kóta and Jokipii, 1997; Kissmann et al., 2003). Eq. (1), in a much simpler form, is also sometimes solved using an analytical ‘force-field’ approach (Gleeson and Axford, 1968). This works well at higher energies (E > 300 MeV). However, as pointed out by Moraal and Steenberg (2001) this approach should not be applied to measurements in the outer heliosphere because it overestimates the adiabatic energy loss. Also greatly expanding our understanding of CR modulation, the structure and extent of the heliosphere during the last two decades are the Pioneer 10 (P10) and 11 (P11), Voyager 1 (V1) and 2 (V2) spacecraft missions. Currently V1 and V2 are at $\sim 86$ and $\sim 68$ AU, respectively, approaching the TS. However, in spite of having these observations and the models at our disposal, many puzzles remain concerning the transport of CRs, particularly for the outer heliosphere. These include our lack of knowing the spatial, temporal and rigidity dependence of all modulation parameters in Eq. (1) (especially diffusion and drifts), as well as the size and shape of the heliosphere, the location and the effects of the TS. These aspects are discussed below. # 3. Cosmic ray diffusion and drifts In Eq. (1) the heliospheric diffusion process are described by the symmetric tensor elements $K_{s}$ and the diffusion coefficients of special interest are: $$
K _ {r r} = K _ {| |} \cos^ {2} \Psi + K _ {\perp r} \sin^ {2} \Psi ; \quad K _ {\theta \theta} = K _ {\perp \theta}, \tag {2}
$$ with $K_{rr}$ is the effective radial coefficient consisting of a parallel diffusion coefficient $K_{||}$ and a perpendicular diffusion coefficient in the radial/azimuthal direction $K_{\perp r}$ , while $K_{\perp \theta}$ is the perpendicular diffusion coefficient in the polar direction. The spiral angle $\Psi$ is defined as the angle between the radial direction and the average HMF. As pointed out by Ferreira et al. (2000) $K_{\perp}$ is more important in the outer heliosphere, especially for the lower energies, than $K_{||}$ . This follows directly from Eq. (2), and although it is usually assumed that $K_{\perp} < K_{||}$ (see discussion below), $K_{\perp}$ dominates $K_{rr}$ in the outer heliosphere where most of the modulation may occur. To determine the exact form of the diffusion coefficients two different but complementing approaches can be used: The turbulence approach.

# A DRIFT MODEL FOR THE MODULATION OF GALACTIC COSMIC RAYS M. S. POTGIETER AND H. MORAAL Department of Physics, Potchefstroom University for C.H.E., Potchefstroom, South Africa Received 1984 October 16; accepted 1985 January 29 # ABSTRACT Detailed numerical solutions of the cosmic-ray transport equation, including drift effects in the interplanetary magnetic field, are presented. This is part of the effort to understand the role and importance of these effects. Jokipii and co-workers have published a series of drift models in this Journal. Their models range from the relatively simple to a full three-dimensional one. Nevertheless, some confusion still exists about the real significance of drift effects. This paper therefore has essentially two purposes. First, our independently developed model generally confirms the results of Jokipii et al. This largely resolves previous questions about discrepancies between independent model predictions. Second, we stress the point that the drift effect and its importance can only be judged by simultaneous simulations of all available modulation features. This restriction has not previously been applied to drift models, and it is shown that it considerably narrows the possible parameter space for modulation conditions. The model successfully simulates proton and electron spectra together with high-energy anisotropies in consecutive solar cycles, using one single set of modulation parameters. Since conventional models experience difficulties in simulating these observations, we conclude that drift should seriously be considered to be related to all modulation phenomena which exhibit a 22 yr cycle. Subject headings: cosmic rays: general — interplanetary medium # I. INTRODUCTION The past eight years have seen a continuing high level of effort to understand the role of drift in cosmic-ray modulation. (See reviews by Jones 1983; Quenby 1984). Jokipii, Levy, and Hubbard (1977) were the first to point out the relative importance of drift effects in the dipolar background interplanetary magnetic field (IMF), which switches its dipole moment approximately every 11 yr. The first numerical models which included drift were reported by Jokipii and Kopriva (1979a, b) and Moraal, Gleeson, and Webb (1979). Both groups concluded that the inclusion of drift in the modulation process produces novel and fundamentally important effects. The predictions of these initial models were, however, contradictory. The model of Moraal, Gleeson, and Webb did not treat the drift in the equatorial neutral sheet properly, while Jokipii and Davila (1981) augmented their earlier results with more realistic diffusion coefficients. Jokipii and Thomas (1981) subsequently incorporated the effect of a wavy neutral sheet into their drift model. Recently, Kóta and Jokipii (1983) developed a full three-dimensional drift model. The revised and extended results of this group have not yet been independently confirmed. In addition, Jokipii and co-workers concentrated mainly on the proton modulation to establish the general effects of drift. The full impact of drift on various other modulation features such as electron spectra, density gradients, anisotropies, and neutron-monitor observations have, however, not yet been established in detail. In this paper we therefore (a) illustrate the general characteristics of the solutions of the steady-state transport equation including drift, (b) show the influence of various parameter variations on these solutions, (c) establish to what extent our model predictions agree with those of Jokipii and Davila (1981), and (d) illustrate that the model can simultaneously, that is, with one single set of parameters, explain modulation features which seem to be related to the reversal of the solar magnetic field. # II. THE DRIFT MODEL Inside the heliosphere the cosmic-ray differential number density $U_{p}(\boldsymbol{r}, p, t)$ with respect to momentum p, position r, and time t, is governed by the equation of transport: $$
\frac {\partial U _ {p}}{\partial t} + \nabla \cdot (V U _ {p} - \mathbf {K} \cdot \nabla U _ {p}) - \frac {1}{3} (\nabla \cdot V) \frac {\partial}{\partial p} (p U _ {p}) = 0, \tag {1}
$$ or, alternatively (see Jokipii, Levy, and Hubbard 1977), $$
\frac {\partial U _ {p}}{\partial t} + \nabla \cdot [ (V + \langle \boldsymbol {v} _ {d} \rangle) U _ {p} - \mathbf {K} ^ {(s)} \cdot \nabla U _ {p} ]
$$ $$
- \frac {1}{3} (\nabla \cdot V) \frac {\partial}{\partial p} (p U _ {p}) = 0. \tag {2}
$$ Here V is the solar wind velocity and $$
\mathbf {K} (\boldsymbol {r}, p, t) = \left| \begin{array}{c c c} K _ {\parallel} & 0 & 0 \\ 0 & K _ {\perp} & K _ {T} \\ 0 & - K _ {T} & K _ {\perp} \end{array} \right| \tag {3}
$$ is the diffusion tensor of the cosmic ray particles in the IMF. The symmetric part of the tensor (3) is denoted by $\mathbf{K}^{(s)}$ . The pitch-angle averaged drift velocity over the near-isotropic particle distribution is given by $$
\langle \boldsymbol {v} _ {d} \rangle = \boldsymbol {\nabla} \times K _ {T} \boldsymbol {e} _ {B}, \tag {4}
$$ with $e_{B}$ the unit vector in the direction of the IMF, denoted by B. The streaming vector or differential current density is given by $$
\boldsymbol {S} _ {p} = C V U _ {p} - \mathbf {K} \cdot \nabla U _ {p}, \tag {5}
$$ or $$
\boldsymbol {S} _ {p} = C V U _ {p} - \mathbf {K} ^ {(s)} \cdot \nabla U _ {p} - K _ {T} \boldsymbol {e} _ {B} \times \nabla U _ {p}. \tag {6}
$$ Here C is the Compton-Getting factor and the term $CVU_{p}$ implicitly includes the electric-field drift velocity $E \times B/B^{2}$ , as shown by Forman and Gleeson (1975). In order to illustrate the relative importance of drift effects on the modulation of cosmic rays, it is necessary to choose all the parameters in equations (1)-(4) as realistic as possible. We therefore discuss them in turn. The radial solar-wind speed used in this model was chosen as a rapidly rising function of radial distance from the surface of the Sun at $r = r_{\odot}$ to a near constant value: $$
V (r) = 4 0 0 [ 1 - \exp {(1 3. 3 (r _ {\odot} - r))} ] \mathrm {km s^ {- 1}}, \tag {7}
$$ where $r$ and $r_{\odot}=0.005$ are expressed in astronomical units (AU). This form is in accord with recent measurements (e.g., Collard, Milhalov, and Wolfe 1982). The standard spiral IMF configuration used in the model is given by $$
\boldsymbol {B} = \frac {B _ {0}}{r ^ {2}} \left(\boldsymbol {e} _ {r} - \tan \psi \boldsymbol {e} _ {\phi}\right), \tag {8}
$$ where $\psi$ is the angle between the direction of the field lines and the radial direction, measured in a heliocentric spherical coordinate system $(r, \theta, \phi)$ . If $\Omega$ is the angular velocity of the Sun about its axis, then $\tan \psi = \Omega(r - r_{\odot}) \sin \theta / V$ . Historically, the most important feature of the IMF concerning the modulation of galactic cosmic rays is not the underlying spiral pattern but rather its small-scale irregularities which scatter cosmic rays in pitch angle. Diffusion theories have not yet been successful to predict the coefficients $K_{\parallel}, K_{\perp}$ , and $K_{T}$ for any general level of turbulence of the IMF (see, e.g., the review by Quenby 1984). Therefore the coefficient $K_{\parallel}$ was chosen by taking into account the following observations and considerations. First, Palmer (1982) summarized all analyses of intensity-time profiles of solar cosmic-ray events to conclude that at Earth the parallel mean free path $\lambda_{\parallel} = 3K_{\parallel}/v$ has a weak rigidity ( $P$ ) dependence below $P \approx 3$ GV and lies in the range $0.08 \lesssim \lambda_{\parallel} \lesssim 0.3$ AU. Second, quasi-linear diffusion theory (QLT) and observations seem to agree that $K_{\perp}/K_{\parallel} \ll 1$ , and therefore the effective radial diffusion coefficient $K_{rr} = K_{\parallel} \cos^2 \psi + K_{\perp} \sin^2 \psi$ may be approximated as $K_{rr} \approx K_{\parallel} \cos^2 \psi$ . Morfill and Völk (1979) and Palmer (1982) agree that near the equatorial plane ( $\theta = \pi/2$ ), $K_{rr}$ is at most very weakly dependent on radial distance $r$ . Third, Morfill and Völk (1979) suggested quite a strong increase of $K_{rr}$ toward the polar regions. Finally, QLT predicts that the rigidity dependence of $K_{\parallel}$ at $P \gtrsim 1$ GV must be of the form $P^a$ with $1 \leq a \leq 2$ , while for $P \lesssim 1$ GV, observations suggest that $a \approx 0$ . Taking into account these observations and predictions, the most acceptable form for $K_{\parallel}$ proved to be $$
K _ {\parallel} = (K _ {\parallel}) _ {0} \left[ 1 + \left(\frac {r}{r _ {e}}\right) ^ {2} \right] \beta K _ {2} (P), \tag {9}
$$ with $\beta = v/c,\quad r_{e} = 1\quad AU,\quad 0.6 \times 10^{22} \leq (K_{\parallel})_{0} \leq 2.25 \times 10^{22}$ $cm^{2}s^{-1}, and$

It is also very possible that the flat H spectrum at P10 in 1987 could be a consequence of their transport along the neutral current sheet. In either case it provides further evidence that adiabatic energy losses may be less than expected. # 7. SOURCE SPECTRA AND THE LOCATION OF THE HELIOSPHERIC BOUNDARY To estimate the residual modulation and the distance to the interstellar boundary, it is necessary to have a local interstellar reference spectrum for one of the observed components. We have selected He$^{++}$ because of its high rigidity, the greater accuracy of our measurements in this region, and the availability of realistic estimates of the local interstellar (LIS) He$^{++}$ spectra. Webber *et al.* [1987] have used the results from a 1976 balloon flight of the New Mexico State University superconducting magnet experiment that covered the rigidity range 5–200 GV. At rigidities $>3$ GV, a power law in rigidity is obtained with $\gamma_{\text{He}} = -2.70$. It is assumed that this source spectrum extends to lower energies and that modulation effects are negligible in the 100- to 200-GV range. Using a standard “leaky box” propagation model with an escape mean free path $\lambda = 9.64\beta$ ($R < 5$ GV) and with interstellar energy losses, an LIS helium spectrum is obtained [Webber *et al.*, 1987] (Figure 16). The force field approximation is used to estimate the boundary location with $$
J_{\infty}(r_b, R) = J_{10}(42.3, R) \int_{42.3}^{r_b} \frac{CVdr}{K_{rr}(r, R, t)}
$$ and $$
K_{rr} = \frac{CV}{G_r} = \frac{CVr^{0.7}}{0.1}
$$ For 300 MeV/nucleon He, $J_{\infty} = 1.02 \text{ P/m}^2 \text{ s sr MeV}$, while $J(42.3 \text{ AU}) = 0.61 \text{ P/m}^2 \text{ s sr MeV}$, leading to a boundary distance, $r_b = 162 \pm 40$ AU. This force field approximation gives a deceleration parameter $\Phi_{\alpha} = 85$ MeV/nucleon. Seo *et al.* [1991] in a 1987 balloon flight using the same superconducting magnet but with a different detector configuration find a spectral slope of $\gamma_{\text{He}} = -2.68 \pm 0.3$ above 10 GV, but the measured intensity is $\sim 10\text{--}15\%$ below the value of Webber *et al.* [1987]. These lower values would move the boundary to $\sim 140$ AU. The differences between these two intensity determinations are regarded as a measure of the uncertainty in determining the LIS He$^{++}$ intensity. The estimates of the boundary location are completely dependent on the accuracy of the determination of the local interstellar He$^{++}$ spectrum as well as on the simplifying assumption regarding the modulation process. McDonald *et al.* [1987] obtained a boundary estimate based on the fact that the long-term decrease in cycle 21 occurred in a series of discrete steps [McDonald *et al.*, 1981] that were correlated with the passage of merged interaction regions (MIRs) [Burlaga *et al.*, 1985]. These MIRs should persist as turbulent pressure waves that may be the major contributor in forming the outer modulation region around solar maximum. Assuming these pressure waves are effective out to the modulation boundary, a crude estimate of the modulation region was made [McDonald *et al.*, 1986] using Fig. 16. The Webber *et al.* local interstellar LIS galactic cosmic ray He spectra compared with the Pioneer (41.3 AU) and IMP 8 (1 AU) 1987 measurements. <!-- page 13 -->
the time interval between the first major intensity decrease in March 1979 (the first step decrease in May 1978 was smaller) and the initial recovery or "reset phase" that began in December 1980 which gave a boundary location of 160 AU. If the modulation region extends beyond the termination shock, there will be a significant decrease in these estimates due to the approximately fourfold reduction in the solar wind velocity as it crosses the shock region. Previous estimates of $r_b$ from the analysis of various P10 and P11 energetic particle integral rates (ions >70 MeV) in low-energy H data have placed the modulation boundary in the range 45-80 AU [Webber, 1987; McKibben et al., 1982; Van Allen, 1988]. McNutt [1988] has invoked traveling interplanetary disturbances as a source of the kilohertz radial emission detected by Kurth et al. [1984] and estimated the boundary distance in the 70-130 AU range. In a recent analysis using the latest measurements of the local interstellar medium, Suess [1990] has shown that the boundary location is mainly determined by the dynamic pressure balance between the solar wind and the LIS magnetic field $B_i$. A value of $B_i = 0.3$ MT gives $r_b = 130$ AU. An increase of $B_i$ to 0.5 MT reduces $r_b$ to 80 AU. The actual uncertainty in $B_i$ is so large that it will perhaps be best determined by independent estimates of $r_b$. The estimates in this paper place $B_i$ on the order of 0.25 MT. ## 8. CONCLUSION There are two major conclusions from this comparative study of cosmic rays at successive solar minima: 1. Drift effects are important in the cosmic ray process, especially for $qA < 0$ solar minimum periods, and the long-term modulation has a 22-year period. 2. The flat hydrogen spectra and the modulation parameters calculated from the force field approximation are not consistent with the conventional expectations of modulation theory. We have tentatively interpreted this as an indication that adiabatic energy losses are not important in the outer heliosphere. The very different time histories of the galactic cosmic ray H and He for cycles 20 and 21, the large negative latitudinal gradients, the suppression of low-rigidity particles, and the shift in the peak intensity of $He^+$ to higher energies in 1987 are strong arguments for the importance of gradient and curvature drifts near solar minimum for those cycles with $qA < 0$. However, the magnitude of the observed effects is much smaller than that predicted using the standard Parker model of the interplanetary magnetic field. The observations are in reasonable agreement with the suggestions by Potgieter et al. [1989] that drift effects should be scaled down by an order of magnitude in the $qA > 0$ cycle. Recently, Le Roux [1990] has shown that a change of this magnitude could be obtained using the Jokipii and Kota [1989] modification of the heliospheric polar magnetic field. The flat H spectra (Figure 15) and the breakdown in the force field approximation (Figure 11) are interpreted in terms of a reduced adiabatic energy loss in the outer heliosphere. Such a reduction could be a reflection of the particle transport along the current sheet. However, the flat H spectra observed as close as 1.8 AU in 1977 indicate that this is a problem for both cycles. The second paper in this series will treat the modulation as a simple, steady state nondrift process. Although it is generally appreciated that such a simple model can fit almost any given single observation in the heliosphere, no attempt has been made since that of Gleeson and Urch [1972] to make a comprehensive simultaneous study of this nature using the available cosmic ray spectra. It is all the more important now, because we have multiple observations from widely different positions in the heliosphere. Then drifts will be added to the steady state modulation process in an attempt to settle the long-standing question of how important or, alternatively, how necessary these drift effects are to achieve improved fits to the observations. The next step is to use the time-dependent version of the numerical solution of the cosmic ray transport equation developed by Le Roux and Potgieter [1990] to explore the importance of time-dependent effects. With the large increase in heliocentric distances of P10 and V1/V2 and the predicted positive latitudinal gradients and large relative enhancement of low-rigidity particles, the solar minimum period of cycle 22 should be of special value in extending these studies. *Acknowledgments.* The Editor thanks J. Hoeksema and J. A. Lockwood for their assistance in evaluating this paper.

